**The Table Is Big Enough**

Friends, neighbors, parents, coaches, teachers:

We are told that division is simply human nature. That children will sort themselves by color, by creed, by zip code, by party, and that nothing can be done. Today I say: look closer at the evidence. Sorting is not destiny. It is design. And what was designed can be redesigned.

The research is plain. You do not make friends by sitting near someone. You make friends by building something together, by losing a game together, by painting the same set and sweating through the same rehearsal. A single season of shared purpose does more than a hundred assemblies about tolerance.

So I have a dream.

I have a dream that a girl whose family fled across an ocean, who prays in a mosque and speaks two languages, will spend her afternoons not only among her own people but also on a robotics team, a soccer field, a stage. And that she will keep both worlds. Her community will not be dissolved. It will be a home she walks out of and returns to, with friends on both sides of the door.

I have a dream that the boy in the mansion and the boy in the apartment will pass the same ball, and that neither will feel like a guest.

I have a dream that a child from a conservative church and a child from a progressive household will build a bridge for the science fair, and discover that the other is a person before they ever learn the other is a position.

I have a dream that we stop counting hours in front of screens that sort us and start counting hours at tables that mix us. Because when we move a few more hours of a child's week into rooms where people share a goal, the share of friendships that cross our lines can nearly double. Not through speeches. Through Tuesday practice.

But hear me. This road runs both ways. We cannot ask the newcomer to cross the whole bridge alone. The welcome must meet them halfway: the coach who learns a name, the family who sends the first invitation, the club that opens its door before it is asked.

And do not mistake mixing for forcing. People rebuild walls that are built around them. So we do not command friendship. We make it easy, free, and close to home. We pay for the bus. We waive the fee. We put the field where all the children can reach it.

I have a dream that when today's children are grown, their wedding guest lists, their group chats, their dinner tables will look like the country we keep promising we are. Not because someone forced them, but because someone built the places where they could meet.

That future is not a prediction. It is a budget, a schedule, a choice. It costs about one percent of what we already spend on schooling. We have the money. We have the evidence. What we need now is the will.

So build the teams. Open the doors. Fill the bench with every kind of child. And let us see, in one generation, what happens when the table is finally big enough for everyone to sit down.

Thank you.

